# Restore the ROCm support NIXL v1.5.2 removed from its LIBFABRIC backend.
#
# VRAM over libfabric worked on v1.5.0 and does not on v1.5.2. The symptom is
# not a transfer failure -- registerMem refuses the segment before libfabric
# is ever asked:
#
#   libfabric_backend.cpp] Memory type VRAM_SEG is not supported by libfabric
#                          backend.
#   nixl_agent.cpp] registerMem: registration failed for the specified or all
#                   potential backends
#
# and the plugin logs "System runtime: SYSTEM (no accelerators)" at startup on
# a guest with two working GPUs, which reads like a broken ROCm install rather
# than deleted code.
#
# WHAT WAS REMOVED
#
# Not one gate -- the whole ROCm path, across five files:
#
#   libfabric_topology.h       6 AMD references -> 0   (isAmdAccel,
#                                                        getNumAmdAccel,
#                                                        num_amd_accel, and the
#                                                        ROCR arm of
#                                                        getMrAttrIface)
#   libfabric_topology.cpp    15 -> 0   (AMD vendor detection and counting)
#   libfabric_rail.cpp         8 -> 0   (mr_attr.device.rocr)
#   libfabric_rail_manager.cpp 3 -> 0   (runtime_ = FI_HMEM_ROCR)
#   libfabric_backend.cpp      6 -> 0   (the FI_HMEM_ROCR arms)
#
# The chain runs bottom-up, which is why restoring any one file alone changes
# nothing: topology detects an AMD GPU by PCI vendor, the rail manager turns a
# non-zero count into FI_HMEM_ROCR, and only then do the backend's predicates
# admit VRAM_SEG. Restoring just the backend leaves it comparing against a
# value nothing produces.
#
# WHY IT WAS PROBABLY REMOVED, AND WHY IT IS SAFE TO PUT BACK HERE
#
# v1.5.2 also reverted its own libfabric build baseline from 2.7.0 to 1.21.0
# (ai-dynamo/nixl#2528). `fi_mr_attr.device.rocr` arrived in libfabric v2.3,
# so on a 1.21.0 baseline the ROCr registration path cannot compile as
# written. Dropping ROCm is consistent with that; nothing in the release notes
# says so.
#
# This repo builds libfabric 2.7.0 (see docker/scripts/build-libfabric.sh), so
# the version the removal was made for does not apply. The restored
# registration keeps v1.5.0's `FI_VERSION >= (2, 3)` guard, so it still
# compiles against an older libfabric -- it simply leaves the device handle
# zeroed there, which is what v1.5.0 did.
#
# Restored rather than reverted: v1.5.2's libfabric_backend.cpp differs from
# v1.5.0's by ~200 lines, and the rest of that is the concurrency work this
# repo wants -- per-rail endpoint locking (#2542) and the EFA
# unsolicited-write fix (#2529).
#
# The AMD class check accepts 0x1200 (Processing accelerators) as well as the
# 0x300-0x3ff display-controller range, because MI300X and the emulated
# rocjitsu both present 0x1200. A check written only against consumer GPUs
# finds nothing on either.
#
# `is_cuda_vram` keeps its name: local, used three lines later, and renaming
# it would enlarge the patch for no behavioural gain. It now means "VRAM on an
# accelerator runtime", CUDA or ROCr.
#
# Drop this when upstream restores ROCm support; check getNumAmdAccel in
# src/utils/libfabric/libfabric_topology.h first, since everything else
# depends on it.
#
# THE BUILD-TIME GATE, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY DECIDES
#
# Restoring the runtime detection above is necessary and still not enough.
# src/plugins/libfabric/meson.build adds -DHAVE_CUDA only when a CUDA toolkit
# is found, and EVERY VRAM path in the backend sits behind that macro. On a
# ROCm machine there is no CUDA, so the whole block is compiled out and
# getSupportedMems() cannot return VRAM_SEG no matter what the rail manager
# detected -- the plugin cheerfully reports "System runtime: ROCr for 2 AMD
# GPU(s)" and then refuses the segment two calls later. That is also why this
# never worked on v1.5.0 either: its ROCR arm is inside the same #ifdef.
#
# So the patch adds -DHAVE_ROCM, keyed off the existing rocm_path option, and
# widens only the support predicate to accept either GPU stack.
#
# The CUDA address workaround -- vramInitCtx, vramUpdateCtx, vramApplyCtx and
# the is_cuda_vram gating in the transfer path -- is deliberately NOT widened.
# It exists to fix a CUDA context problem, ROCr has no equivalent, and routing
# ROCr through it would call CUDA driver entry points on a machine with no
# CUDA. An earlier draft of this patch did exactly that.
#
# No ROCm dependency is linked, because the ROCr path needs no HIP calls from
# this plugin: the device handle is set in the rail layer via
# fi_mr_attr.device.rocr.
#
